{"assets":["/editorial-assets/4ef578027c5a26394402738545ec04660c411bff474d218d34e17214f0d2acd1.png","/editorial-assets/257d3220cb7a1da4e60922a175eeae22b07978d0caed567399b3b3d38e405b84.png"],"contestId":"454","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Overview"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are 3 normal tasks accompanied with 2 challenge tasks in div 1 as we usually do. You can check the "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/13271"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Statistics"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" by By "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/DmitriyH"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"DmitriyH"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" for detail."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem B, C is by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/sevenkplus"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"sevenkplus"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", problem D is by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/xlk"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"xlk"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" and problem A, E is by me. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem E is rather complicated then hard, it means if you carefully broke down the problem into smaller ones, it became quite straightforward. During the contest, only "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/kcm1700"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"kcm1700"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" managed to solve it successfully. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem D, which decided the round boiled down to the following nice Dynamic Programming subproblem: you are given 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"20"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" numbers. For each position i between 0 and 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"20"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, and for each distance "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" between 0 and 20, what is the sum of the numbers with such indexes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" differ in exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" bits? The fun part is not how to do it "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"T"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" times, it is how to do it even once on 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" numbers."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Petr"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Petr"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" solve A, B, C, D steadily and fast who indisputably, is the winner of the round. The second place came to "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/msg555"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"msg555"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", closely followed by "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/cgy4ever"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cgy4ever"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":". It is worth mentioning that, "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/cgy4ever"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cgy4ever"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" solved Problem D in the last few seconds which is quite impressive."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":1},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Editorial"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/454/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem 2A. Little Pony and Crystal Mine"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Draw the grid graph as the problem said. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Just a few basics of your programming language. It's easy."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/454/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem 2B. Little Pony and Sort by Shift"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Ask the minimum unit shift you need to sort a array."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Just a few basics of your programming language. It's not hard."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/453/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem A. Little Pony and Expected Maximum"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Calculate the expected maximum number after tossing a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" faces dice "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" times."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Take "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 6, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2 as a instance."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"6 6 6 6 6 6\n5 5 5 5 5 6\n4 4 4 4 5 6\n3 3 3 4 5 6\n2 2 3 4 5 6\n1 2 3 4 5 6\n"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Enumerate the maximum number, the distribution will be a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-dimensional super-cube with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-length-side. Each layer will be a large cube minus a smaller cube. So we have:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4ef578027c5a26394402738545ec04660c411bff474d218d34e17214f0d2acd1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Calculate "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" may cause overflow, we could move the divisor into the sum and calculate ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" instead."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/453/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem B. Little Pony and Harmony Chest"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"You are given sequence "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", find a pairwise coprime sequence "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" which minimizes "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/257d3220cb7a1da4e60922a175eeae22b07978d0caed567399b3b3d38e405b84.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Since {1, 1 ..., 1} is a pairwise coprime sequence, the maximum element of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" can never greater then 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"mx"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1. Here "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"mx"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the maximum elements in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". So what we need consider is the first a few prime factors. It is not hard to use bitmask-dp to solve this:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"    for (int i = 1 ; i <= n ; i ++) {\n    \tfor (int k = 1 ; k < 60 ; k ++) {\n    \t\tint x = (~fact[k]) & ((1 << 17) - 1);\n    \t\tfor (int s = x ; ; s = (s - 1) & x) {\n                if (dp[i - 1][s] + abs(a[i] - k) < dp[i][s | fact[k]]){\n                    dp[i][s | fact[k]] = dp[i-1][s] + abs(a[i]-k);\n                }\n    \t\tif (s == 0) break;\n            }\n    \t}\n    }\n"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Here "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dp[i][s]"}],"kind":"inline_code"},{"kind":"text","text":": means the first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" items of the sequence, and the prime factor have already existed. And "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"fact[k]"}],"kind":"inline_code"},{"kind":"text","text":"： means the prime factor set of number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/453/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem C. Little Pony and Summer Sun Celebration"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Given a undirected graph with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" nodes and the parity of the number of times that each place is visited. Construct a consistent path."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There is no solution if there is more than 1 connected component which have odd node (because we can't move between two component), otherwise it is always solvable."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This fact is not obvious, let's focus on one component. You can select any node to start, denoted it as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (root). Start from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", you can go to any other odd node then back. Each time you can eliminate one odd node. After that, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" itself is odd, you can simply delete the first or last element in your path (it must be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The only spot of the above method is the size of the path can been large as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). We need a more local observation. Let's check the following dfs() function:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"void dfs(int u = r, int p = -1){\n    \n    vis[u] = true;\n    add_to_path(u);\n\n    for_each(v in adj[u]) if (!vis[v]){\n        dfs(v, u);\n        add_to_path(u);\n    }\n\n    if (odd[u] && p != -1){\n        add_to_path(p);\n        add_to_path(u);\n    }\n}\n"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"dfs()"}],"kind":"inline_code"},{"kind":"text","text":" maintain the following loop invariant: before we leave a node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we clear all odd node in the sub-tree rooted at "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as well as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" itself. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The only "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can break the invariant is the root itself. So after dfs(), we use O(1) time to check weather root is still a odd node, if yes, delete the first or last element of the path (it must be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After that, all the node will been clear, each node can involve at most 4 items in the path. So the size of the path will less than or equal to 4"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Thus the overall complexity is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/453/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem D. Little Pony and Elements of Harmony"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Given you a vector "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"e"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and a transformation matrix "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Caculate "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"eA"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" under modulo "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's consider the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"e"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = [1 1 ... 1]. After a period, it will be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ke"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a const. So we know that [1 1, ..., 1] is an eigenvector and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the corresponding an eigenvalue."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The linear transformation has 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" eigenvectors. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(0 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")-th "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"eigenvector is [(-1)^f(0, i) (-1)^f(1, i) ... (-1)^f(2^m-1, i)], where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") means that the number of ones in the binary notation of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We notice that the eigenvalue is only related to the number of ones in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and it is not hard to calc one eigenvalue in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time. To decompose the initial vector to the eigenvectors, we need "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_Walsh%E2%80%93Hadamard_transform"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Fast Walsh–Hadamard transform"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also check SRM 518 1000 for how to use FWT. "},{"attrs":{"href":"http://apps.topcoder.com/wiki/display/tc/SRM+518"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"http://apps.topcoder.com/wiki/display/tc/SRM+518"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the last step, we need divide n. We can mod (p * n) in the precedure and divide n directly."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/453/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem E. Little Pony and Lord Tirek"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Brief description:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ponies (from 1 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") in a line, each pony has:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":": the start mana."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":": the maximum mana."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":": mana regeneration per unit time."}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Also, you have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" operations called Absorb Mana "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". For each operations, at time "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", count how many mana from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". After that, reset each pony's mana to 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Analysis:"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"level":5},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Key Observation"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The income of a operation, is only relevant with the previous operation. In other words, what we focus on is the difference time between adjacent operations."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":5},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Weaken the problem"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let us assume "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1 at the beginning to avoid disrupting when we try to find the main direction of the algorithm. Also it will be much easier if the problem only ask the sum of all query. One of the accepted method is following:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Firstly, for each operation ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), we split it into a insert event on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and a delete event "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. Secondly, we use scanning from left to right to accumulate the contributions of each pony."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In order to do that, you need a balanced tree to maintenance the difference time between adjacent operations, and a second balanced tree to maintenance some kind of prefixes sum according to those \"difference\"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first balanced tree could been implemented by STL::SET. For each operation, you need only constant insert and delete operations on those balanced tree, thus the overall complexity is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nlogn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":5},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"General solution"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Instead of scanning, now we use a balanced tree to maintenance the intervals which have same previous operation time and use a functional interval tree to maintenance those ponies. For each operation, we use binary search on the first balanced tree, and query on the second balanced tree. Thus the overall complexity is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nlog"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/13190"}